#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157031 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "bb5306eb-02c5-52a3-b7b0-92610059b065" :question/math-academy-id "e-15064" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = ( u + v)^2, \\qquad y = \\dfrac{u}{v+1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side $S_1$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_1.$\n\nFor the side $S_1,$ we have $v = 0$ and $0 \\leq u \\leq 1.$ Substituting $v = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =((u+v)^{2},\\,\\frac{u}{v+1}) \\\\  & =((u+0)^{2},\\,\\frac{u}{0+1}) \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_1$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx = u^2, \\qquad y = u, \\qquad 0 \\leq u \\leq 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t^2, \\qquad y(t) = t, \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny^2=x\n$$\n\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of $S_1$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,0)\\to ((0+0)^{2},\\,\\frac{0}{0+1})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(1,0)\\to ((1+0)^{2},\\,\\frac{1}{0+1})=(1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $S_1$ is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$\n\n![](images/7f/7f94f6fef873efd64d9da9e015e0056944b63846d741e12937507205b118638b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157032 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157255 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"}] :question/id #uuid "d08f8e35-c4e7-5eef-aca5-175585760433" :question/math-academy-id "e-15065" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = uv, \\qquad y = u(u+v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points in the interior of $\\Delta.$ So, to map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $\\Delta$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_1,$ where we have $v = 0$ and $0 \\leq u \\leq 1.$ Substituting $v = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(u(0),\\,u(u+0)) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_2.$ Here, we have $u = 1$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 1.$ Substituting $u = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(1(v),\\,1(1+v)) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1+v), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_3.$ Here, we have $v = 1$ and $1 \\geq u \\geq 0.$ Substituting $v = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(u(1),\\,u(u+1)) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,{u}^{2}+u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_4.$ Here, we have $u = 0$ and $1 \\geq v \\geq 0.$ Substituting $u = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(0v,\\,0(0+v)) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $\\Delta{:}$\n\n![](images/79/793412e250ac4f26966293771306f28bb037685dd7886dc386706f23dcaee8af.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157033 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d96918f5-2a27-5413-8fcd-a6c8060a9342" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Segment"}] :question/id #uuid "f35926af-6c5f-537f-ab22-ec2d62995267" :question/math-academy-id "e-15066" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ and its **directed** boundary $\\partial\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, where $\\Delta$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = 2u-3v, \\qquad y=v^2-3u.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side $S_3$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_3.$\n\nFor the side $S_3,$ we have $v=1$ and $1 \\geq u \\geq 0.$ Substituting $v = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(2u-3v,\\,{v}^{2}-3u) \\\\  & =(2u-3(1),\\,{1}^{2}-3u) \\\\  & =(2u-3,1-3u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_3$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2u-3, \\qquad y=1-3u, \\qquad 1 \\geq u \\geq 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = 2t-3, \\qquad y(t) = 1-3t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,-2)$ and $(-3,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/15/1583f8d3ad69b8d5e3ac051eeb50b55860737a60d462d126ae5dc2b49c3ea155.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157034 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf2d579-5da0-5061-94f2-eec22eae7902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of an Image"}] :question/id #uuid "ec73a2b5-8f4a-5418-983d-b35013387844" :question/math-academy-id "e-15087" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = u - v^2, \\qquad y = 2v.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side $S_4$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{x(t)}, \\qquad y=2t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_4.$\n\nFor the side $S_4,$ we have $u = 0$ and $1 \\geq v \\geq 0.$ Substituting $u = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u-{v}^{2},\\,2v) \\\\  & =(0-{v}^{2},\\,2v) \\\\  & =(-{v}^{2},\\,2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_4$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx = -v^2, \\qquad y=2v, \\qquad 1 \\geq v \\geq 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-t^2}, \\qquad y(t)=2t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157255 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "600a43db-7ad7-59a7-8a4e-811756e9e090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/43/4367227477398d54e05d978d1499aea7f0dabb42ab9223b21cbec43c5ada55c2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389030} {:answer/id #uuid "af57ed6d-87af-5a9e-aba0-d344b0beded2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/12/12ded9e8002035e126364e41d0dffc1abd6332b3496b4db9da4a68e116e7492e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389031} {:answer/id #uuid "f1817307-acca-5145-b9f8-f957482ffe59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/468a2bf9e981f18b656fe1b5a023f98d1f5c990880caf5f9908c05cc3fe2d197.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389032} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e1dfd6-e586-58b6-a3b2-fb619c6f4782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/25/25f5503b720084033be843eb1bce7adfe3bc59c39f5f634074d4e414079c9c36.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389033} {:answer/id #uuid "15d16791-9d02-5cda-8ca2-b435a7d594dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/33/33293e18f3b42154b3a21dd627e7f06fbcf443b1251ac1a9ce6e5cb6a47c5971.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1817307-acca-5145-b9f8-f957482ffe59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/468a2bf9e981f18b656fe1b5a023f98d1f5c990880caf5f9908c05cc3fe2d197.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389032} :answer-field/id #uuid "318f82be-54f2-5f7d-8b28-47015fa276c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91008bb7-2af5-5c56-89d5-09e110f3df57" :question/math-academy-id "q-160353" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y=u(1+{v}^{2}).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,u(1+{0}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,u), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1+{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(1,\\,u(1+{1}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(1,\\,2u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,0(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/46/468a2bf9e981f18b656fe1b5a023f98d1f5c990880caf5f9908c05cc3fe2d197.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf2d579-5da0-5061-94f2-eec22eae7902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00826534-9ddb-597e-a5b4-4a93def9bd22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407210} {:answer/id #uuid "1402d9d4-6fb9-5711-a369-724d5ea71016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407212} {:answer/id #uuid "924df19c-bb6c-5527-89a8-97aeec9e95f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407223} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c147ca-5893-5b85-a3fd-b0d1b30cb874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407225} {:answer/id #uuid "e270c150-b9e2-53a0-a3c8-c86305ab1238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "924df19c-bb6c-5527-89a8-97aeec9e95f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407223} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c2bcbff-1030-59ca-9fb9-26027999b654" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b1e6ffe-be0a-5264-9741-046fc4e7d069" :question/math-academy-id "q-160357" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y=u(1+{v}^{2}).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=y(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nFind the function $y(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) \\\\  & =(v,\\,1(1+{v}^{2})) \\\\  & =(v,1+{v}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y=1+{v}^{2},\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=1+{t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157618 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157255 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4589a8a7-f051-572e-a25b-cba772b96ff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/41e864d2554c7007d3e23e7121ac6552cf6a33563b107f71e7d12d43ac7e58bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407216} {:answer/id #uuid "4df51f26-e472-5a52-9d84-60f233e16ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1f/1fbc53395062b956578f583c12f02ec45544eaba9365986e83343cf4ff648167.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407217} {:answer/id #uuid "585890c6-349d-5e4b-9e94-a9bab08010cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8c/8ce9ccb7a714291aaeb31440d0476569355938f79e3aea0bf30c298e5fa8e311.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407220} {:answer/id #uuid "d56a8ed2-f8fb-51b4-ad31-de2d59c40721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8bf57b257c2e47d8927e6a441740b503ff4e01931989ee2820f6cfcbec1f8d53.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407228} {:answer/id #uuid "d65a3cae-36d7-5c36-ad61-74f27433b1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/af1a848098711c212031b4559bd496773464a93d192c367b0f7a1c847a1ee15b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4589a8a7-f051-572e-a25b-cba772b96ff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/41e864d2554c7007d3e23e7121ac6552cf6a33563b107f71e7d12d43ac7e58bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407216} :answer-field/id #uuid "3008a3ae-1a0a-5c5c-aeb0-57e78911dec0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ae05696-5f01-5ceb-ade9-b9460da7e939" :question/math-academy-id "q-160570" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=uv,\\,y={u}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u\\cdot 0,\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1\\cdot v,\\,{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u\\cdot 1,\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,{u}^{2}), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0\\cdot v,\\,{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n \n\n![](images/68/6812a33686ab2647cd0f5d586152c68770ebfcb1b8d85cedd98f6839a8364afd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c7985e0-0857-5804-a91d-6874ee7f39a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275588} {:answer/id #uuid "34415490-023d-5c18-bba7-038451023e67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275947} {:answer/id #uuid "6e076705-7458-5ed0-8526-2a222bbe61f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276467} {:answer/id #uuid "d588ca3a-96a4-544f-90a5-ba156834c738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,2)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277364} {:answer/id #uuid "f885127e-36b3-5abe-b21f-1dd3b715c6b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from  $(1,0)$ to $(2,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c7985e0-0857-5804-a91d-6874ee7f39a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275588} :answer-field/id #uuid "eefec963-55e6-5c35-9531-076352440923" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "235488a1-420f-5270-8713-f985503b0b2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-160607" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u(u+v),\\,y=\\frac{2u}{v+1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u(u+v),\\,\\frac{2u}{v+1}) \\\\  & =(u(u+0),\\,\\frac{2u}{0+1}) \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,2u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx={u}^{2},\\,y=2u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2},\\,y(t)=2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx={(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{2}=4x\n$$\n\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{1}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,0)\\to (0(0+0),\\,\\frac{2(0)}{0+1})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(1,0)\\to (1(1+0),\\,\\frac{2(1)}{0+1})=(1,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{1}$ is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2).$\n\n![](images/4f/4fc3c209e83fc1d6b4c9258cdb739fb0a73db797791cf8fcca2e51a7592d54f8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf2d579-5da0-5061-94f2-eec22eae7902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2dcac8d-e192-52d2-a860-f232f64707c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407226} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1c4bec-d2d1-5ebd-aec5-afc647b5dca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407227} {:answer/id #uuid "dc27fb6b-147f-5701-a343-70552c33d5c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407230} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7efbbb-13d6-52d4-bb72-add345cc6fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407231} {:answer/id #uuid "e504c718-1387-5ae2-8eea-bdebd385c5db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca1c4bec-d2d1-5ebd-aec5-afc647b5dca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407227} :answer-field/id #uuid "2aab39e5-3823-5bb8-b773-c98978518f2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d45fe3c2-157b-597a-b7a4-36ff992f60f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-225018" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v+2u,\\,y=u({v}^{2}-1).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,y=y(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nFind the function $y(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $v=0.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v+2u,\\,u({v}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =(0+2u,\\,u(0-1)) \\\\  & =(2u,-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2u,\\,y=-u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2t,\\,y(t)=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d96918f5-2a27-5413-8fcd-a6c8060a9342" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a303dc-f918-50e3-813e-224b2d5ada75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(0,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288043} {:answer/id #uuid "19cd60b3-89d9-5b97-ba07-e247159970b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288203} {:answer/id #uuid "6040ba21-9a76-5266-a8cc-6d6a36605c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288710} {:answer/id #uuid "8548c197-3752-5a10-9f54-e93e5066160c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288938} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5a7403-cdae-5fd1-9063-fa942b91c2fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8548c197-3752-5a10-9f54-e93e5066160c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288938} :answer-field/id #uuid "02414deb-2129-5a8c-92f0-e0e35d8fc56b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b39b2a59-ee1b-58ec-89c3-bea4b6d6a4dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-225035" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v-u,\\,y={v}^{3}+u.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v-u,\\,{v}^{3}+u) \\\\  & =(0-u,\\,{0}^{3}+u) \\\\  & =(-u,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=-u,\\,y=u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-t,\\,y(t)=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(-1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/fc/fc5daa3ee483ed2a7e78d30a247c6d4c9ae4d77691d5d5659c0b9ac2fdf6621a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d96918f5-2a27-5413-8fcd-a6c8060a9342" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e26268d-49a1-5a83-8429-e2dbae2639b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(2,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407213} {:answer/id #uuid "5165e145-830c-56a0-9173-fcfbf2800e9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0)$ and $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407218} {:answer/id #uuid "524c3a00-a206-5dde-a568-5a05da724097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407219} {:answer/id #uuid "87c16c9a-0fa3-5130-8d05-8c94f59b0ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407222} {:answer/id #uuid "b16f1a1f-9a49-5973-9be2-b7ebcc061568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b16f1a1f-9a49-5973-9be2-b7ebcc061568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407224} :answer-field/id #uuid "07fdf1dc-3a9e-518a-9121-2ac6a2492963" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c666819-f94c-5831-9c13-86415b44c537" :question/math-academy-id "q-225047" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+2v,\\,y=v-2u.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+2v,\\,v-2u) \\\\  & =({0}^{2}+2v,\\,v-2(0)) \\\\  & =(2v,v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2v,\\,y=v,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2t,\\,y(t)=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(2,1)$ and $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/0b/0b61f3a91c912dcd042bdd5cc11b0f393a8ea85269ecbef000403cf080166cf7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f3dd863-4c20-526b-baf8-1dd1aaa12685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,-1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407211} {:answer/id #uuid "339db576-bc8c-5b76-9e7a-ca3861bc68aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407214} {:answer/id #uuid "44ffdafc-3200-56d7-8223-b6163e910bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,-1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407215} {:answer/id #uuid "64581119-0bb0-5b9d-9448-7470ca81dcea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,-1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407221} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fa2dfd-1a44-5050-a80a-0ba6f29b907d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44ffdafc-3200-56d7-8223-b6163e910bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,-1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407215} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d7f7c06-e324-58f6-9f45-6e2ead76ed4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49191016-a732-5d67-86a5-31f2333ebbd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-225169" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}{v}^{2},\\,y=u-v.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}{v}^{2},\\,u-v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2},\\,u-1) \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx={u}^{2},\\,y=u-1,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2},\\,y(t)=t-1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx=(y+1)^{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,-1).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{3}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,1)\\to ({1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2},\\,1-1)=(1,0) \\\\  & T:(0,1)\\to ({0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2},\\,0-1)=(0,-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{3}$ is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,-1).$\n\n![](images/d8/d8861cf6be2cb40702ec6f97d75dbb55e50cdde32a5152428dcdeecf839f270a.png)"}]}
